A frequent live-coding prompt: model a traffic light so an invalid state — like "red and green both on" — can't compile.

```typescript
type TrafficLight =
  | { state: "red" }
  | { state: "yellow" }
  | { state: "green" };

function next(light: TrafficLight): TrafficLight {
  switch (light.state) {
    case "red": return { state: "green" };
    case "green": return { state: "yellow" };
    case "yellow": return { state: "red" };
    // TypeScript's exhaustiveness check flags a missing case here
  }
}
```

The crucial design choice is that `TrafficLight` is a sum type with exactly one active state per value — there's no `{red: true, green: true}` shape available to construct, because the type simply doesn't have independent boolean fields for each color. Compare that to `{ red: boolean, yellow: boolean, green: boolean }`, which lets you build seven illegal combinations (all-off, all-on, any two at once) that the traffic-light domain says can never actually happen.
